{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-sonnet-4-6","id":"msg_017K84Fs47HTMC1kkfjfruWD","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"The user wants to replace the custom `DoublyLinkedList` with Java's built-in `LinkedHashMap`. \n\n`LinkedHashMap` has a built-in LRU mechanism when constructed with `accessOrder=true`. It also has a `removeEldestEntry` hook that can be overridden to automatically evict the oldest entry when the map exceeds capacity.\n\nWith `LinkedHashMap(capacity, loadFactor, accessOrder=true)`:\n- `get` automatically moves the accessed entry to the end (most recently used)\n- `put` adds new entries at the end\n- The eldest (least recently used) entry is at the front\n\nSo the refactored `LRUCache<K, V>` would:\n- Eliminate the `Node` and `DoublyLinkedList` classes entirely\n- Use `LinkedHashMap<K, V>` as the internal data structure\n- Override `removeEldestEntry` to enforce the capacity limit\n\nWriting the refactored implementation... I'm realizing that `LinkedHashMap` with `accessOrder=true` automatically handles the access tracking on both `get` and `put` operations, so I can simplify this significantly by removing the custom `Node` and `DoublyLinkedList` classes entirely and just leveraging the built-in LRU behavior with `removeEldestEntry`.\n\nWriting the refactored LRU cache... Finishing the test cases...","signature":""}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":3,"cache_creation_input_tokens":17246,"cache_read_input_tokens":15934,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":17246},"output_tokens":7,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"diagnostics":{"cache_miss_reason":{"type":"system_changed","cache_missed_input_tokens":14857}},"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"ca89b7eb-916e-4b17-a7e5-94e3caf9db49","uuid":"6cf18034-5f85-451d-bf8e-95d180aeed71","request_id":"req_011Ccjtgqf6dJH